# 2. DEFINE THE FUNDAMENTAL COMPATIBILITY CONTRACT

The new visual editing system must extend the current application without unnecessarily rebuilding it.

Define a formal compatibility contract with the following core principles.

### A. Existing design preservation

Before any administrator edits a page, the new rendering architecture must reproduce the current storefront as closely as possible.

Preserve:

- Section order and conditional visibility.
- Page dimensions and responsive container widths.
- Existing fonts and typography.
- Colors, gradients, and shadows.
- Existing product/category presentation.
- Images and background assets.
- Planet hero videos.
- Celestial scroll effects.
- Animation timing and transitions.
- Navigation structure.
- Footer content and layout.
- Button appearances and interactive states.

Changes to the architecture must not silently redesign the website.

### B. Existing functionality preservation

All current business features must continue working.

### C. Backward compatibility

Existing store settings and database records must remain usable.

### D. Data integrity

Visual customization must not corrupt product, category, or admin data.

### E. Safe editing

Unpublished changes must not affect live customers.

### F. Responsive consistency

Desktop, tablet, and mobile behavior must remain correct.

### G. Security preservation

Visual editing must not weaken authentication, authorization, database permissions, or safe external-link handling.

Define measurable acceptance criteria for each principle.

